course: dictionary
lesson: 10149
title: One word, many meanings: through
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word through has many meanings, so the context tells you which one to use. Ask yourself: what is going through something, or how long does it last?
explain: «Sound travels through air, water and solids, but not through empty space.» Here through means across or within something. The clue is the things sound moves in: air, water and solids.
explain: «She won through hard work.» Here through means by means of, or because of. Try the swap test: put "by means of" into the sentence. It still makes sense, so that is the right meaning.
explain: «I'm through with my homework» means finished. When through describes a time, as in «work through the night», it means from the start to the end.
fact: Through can mean from one side or end to the other, as in «walk through the door».
fact: Through can mean from the start to the end of a time, as in «work through the night».
fact: «Monday through Friday» (US) means from Monday to Friday, including both days, so it is five days.
fact: Through can also mean finished, as in «I'm through with my homework».
fact: A through train goes all the way without changing.
exercise: «Sound travels through air, water and solids, but not through empty space.» Where can sound NOT travel? | *Empty space | Water | Solids | Air
exercise: «The potoo can see through notches in its eyelids.» What does through mean here? | Going on for a long time | Finished with its eyelids | *Looking out using the gaps as a way to see | Very tired
exercise: «Monday through Friday» (US) includes both ends. How many school days is that? | 6 days | 3 days | *5 days | 4 days
test: «She won through hard work.» What does through mean here? | *By means of | Finished | Across | Without
test: «I'm through with my homework.» What does through mean here? | Lost | Going across | *Finished | Still busy
exam: «Most goods cross the sea squeezing through a few narrow passages.» What does through mean here? | Very quickly | Finished with the passages | Because of the passages | *Going from one side to the other, through a gap
